    { "front": "Give the two mechanisms by which upstream diuretics cause potassium wasting.", "back": "(1) Upstream sodium block increases sodium delivery to principal cells, so more Na+ enters through ENaC, the lumen-negative gradient grows, and K+ leaves through ROMK. (2) Volume depletion activates RAAS; aldosterone increases ENaC expression, Na+/K+ ATPase activity and ROMK activity, amplifying the loss.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "How do ENaC inhibitors and MR antagonists both end up sparing potassium?", "back": "Both reduce Na+ entry through ENaC, so the lumen-negative potential diminishes or is abolished and K+ secretion stops. The magnitude of the MR antagonist effect depends on how much aldosterone was present beforehand.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Name the three mechanisms by which NSAIDs blunt diuretics.", "back": "(1) Loss of PGE2/PGI2-mediated afferent dilation causes afferent vasoconstriction, reduced renal blood flow and GFR, and reduced diuretic delivery. (2) Inhibition of OATs reduces secretion of drug into proximal tubular fluid. (3) Direct sodium and water retention.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "How much filtered sodium and bicarbonate does the proximal tubule normally reabsorb, and what does a CAI do to it?", "back": "65-70% of filtered Na+ and 80-90% of filtered bicarbonate. CAIs block both membrane-bound and cytosolic carbonic anhydrase, so H+ is not available for NHE3, and bicarbonate plus sodium are not reabsorbed.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Why are carbonic anhydrase inhibitors weak diuretics despite blocking so much sodium reabsorption?", "back": "The nephron distal to the proximal tubule has a very large sodium reabsorptive capacity. Sodium and water released proximally are reclaimed in the TAL, DCT and collecting ducts, so only a small percentage of filtered sodium is finally excreted.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Why is the CAI diuretic effect self-limited after 2-4 days, and what is NOT self-limited?", "back": "Plasma bicarbonate falls, so filtered bicarbonate falls, so less proximal sodium reabsorption is available to block; simultaneously ECF contraction plus acidosis stimulate aldosterone and ADH, upregulating ENaC and AQP2. The metabolic acidosis is NOT self-limited, because bicarbonate reabsorption stays blocked and buffering capacity is depleted.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "How does acetazolamide treat acute mountain sickness?", "back": "Hypoxia at altitude drives hyperventilation, causing hypocapnia and respiratory alkalosis, which blunts peripheral chemoreceptors and inhibits the respiratory center. Inducing a mild metabolic acidosis overrides that inhibition, increases minute ventilation, sustains hyperventilation, improves oxygenation, and the high-altitude symptoms diminish.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Why is the CAI effect in glaucoma not self-limited?", "back": "Glaucoma treatment does not depend on filtered bicarbonate. CAIs reduce bicarbonate PRODUCTION in the ciliary epithelium, lowering aqueous humor secretion and intraocular pressure, so falling plasma bicarbonate cannot switch the effect off.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Why do CAIs increase the risk of kidney stones, and which crystal?", "back": "Bicarbonate excretion alkalinizes the urine and CAIs also increase urinary calcium (blocked paracellular reabsorption). Alkaline urine plus calcium favors precipitation of calcium phosphate crystals.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "List the contraindications to carbonic anhydrase inhibitors.", "back": "Pre-existing acidosis, severe renal disease, cirrhosis or other severe hepatic disease (risk of hyperammonemia from reduced NH4+ production), and hypersensitivity to the drug or any component of the formulation.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Why do loop diuretics increase calcium and magnesium excretion?", "back": "K+ leaking back into the lumen through ROMK normally creates a lumen-POSITIVE transepithelial potential that drives paracellular Ca2+ and Mg2+ reabsorption. Blocking NKCC2 eliminates that potential, so both divalent cations are excreted along with Na+, K+ and Cl-.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "What is the current teaching on sulfonamide cross-reactivity with diuretics?", "back": "The risk of cross-reactivity in patients with a history of sulfonamide allergy is clinically insignificant, because diuretics lack the N4-aromatic amine responsible for the immune-mediated reaction to sulfonamide antibiotics. Board questions may still expect ethacrynic acid, the only loop without a sulfonamide moiety.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "When and why would you switch a patient from furosemide to torsemide?", "back": "In refractory edema unresponsive to high-dose furosemide, especially heart failure patients with gut edema. Furosemide oral absorption is only about 50-60% and variable; torsemide is nearly 100% absorbed with predictable absorption even in gut edema, plus a longer half-life and duration.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "What are the takeaways for ethacrynic acid?", "back": "It lacks the sulfonamide moiety. It is NOT more effective than the other loop diuretics, has NO pharmacokinetic advantage, carries a HIGHER ototoxicity risk, is very expensive, and availability may be limited.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Explain how loop diuretics abolish BOTH urine concentration and urine dilution.", "back": "The TAL reabsorbs salt without water (it is water-impermeable), which dilutes the tubular fluid and builds the hypertonic medullary interstitium. Blocking NKCC2 leaves solutes in the tubular fluid so urine cannot be diluted below about 200 mOsm/kg, and collapses the medullary gradient so water cannot be reabsorbed from the collecting duct even when vasopressin is present.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "What is the PRIMARY mechanism of loop diuretic resistance with chronic use?", "back": "Distal nephron adaptation - hypertrophy of segments below the TAL with upregulation of NCC, ENaC and Na+/K+ ATPase, increasing downstream sodium reabsorptive capacity. It is amplified by volume depletion driving RAAS, aldosterone and ADH.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Which patients are most susceptible to loop diuretic-refractory edema?", "back": "Heart failure, chronic kidney disease, cirrhosis with ascites, nephrotic syndrome, and secondary hyperaldosteronism.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Give the mechanism of loop diuretic-induced hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis.", "back": "Potassium loss plus increased H+ secretion (both aldosterone-regulated at the principal cell), volume contraction with RAAS activation promoting proximal tubule bicarbonate reabsorption, and chloride loss - hypochloremia impairs bicarbonate excretion while new bicarbonate is generated and retained.", "tag": "Concept" },
    { "front": "Why are loop diuretics ototoxic, and which agent is worst?", "back": "They inhibit NKCC1 in the inner ear, disrupting the endolymph potassium balance needed for normal hearing and balance. Ethacrynic acid has the highest risk; aminoglycosides and cisplatin add synergistic oto- and nephrotoxicity.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Why are thiazides, not loop diuretics, first line for primary hypertension?", "back": "Loops carry a greater risk of volume and electrolyte depletion and have a short duration of action. Thiazides decrease peripheral vascular resistance and produce a sustained blood pressure lowering effect.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "Explain the triple-hit acute kidney injury from an NSAID, an ACE inhibitor and a diuretic.", "back": "The NSAID blocks prostaglandin-mediated afferent dilation so the afferent constricts; the ACE inhibitor/ARB blocks angiotensin II-mediated efferent constriction so the efferent dilates; the diuretic depletes effective circulating volume so renal blood flow falls. Glomerular filtration pressure and GFR collapse, risking acute kidney injury.", "tag": "Clinical" },
    { "front": "What is unique about loop diuretics and renal prostaglandins?", "back": "Loop diuretics directly stimulate intrarenal PGE2 and PGI2 secretion - the other classes do not. This briefly increases renal blood flow and preserves GFR acutely, and it is why NSAIDs blunt loop diuretics particularly hard.", "tag": "Concept" }
